  {
   "type": "done",
   "answer": "No, not all accesses to the same bank cause conflicts. According to the lecture, same-address reads can use broadcast, which avoids a conflict. A conflict (serialization) occurs specifically when different addresses in the same bank are accessed, requiring multiple service rounds [S1, p. 51].\n\n**Reasoning:**\n1.  **Broadcast:** The lecture states that \"Same-address reads can use broadcast\" [S1, p. 51]. This implies that if multiple threads access the exact same address within a bank, the data is broadcast to all of them in a single cycle, so no conflict occurs.\n2.  **Conflict Condition:** The lecture explicitly states that \"Different addresses in the same bank require multiple service rounds\" [S1, p. 51]. This is the condition that causes serialization (a bank conflict).\n\nTherefore, only accesses to *different* addresses within the same bank cause conflicts; accesses to the *same* address do not.\n\n**Source:**\n*   [S1] Page 51: \"Same-address reads can use broadcast. Different addresses in the same bank require multiple service rounds.\"",
   "citations": [
    {
     "document_id": "ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402",
     "chunk_id": "ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402-p51-1",
     "page": 51,
     "text": "GPU Memory Hierarchy\n\nAccess Memory: Bank Conflicts Serialize Otherwise Parallel\nAccesses\n\n\n\n\n\n▪ Typical GPUs use 32 banks; the figure shows 8 for readability. Same-address\n  reads can use broadcast.\n▪ Different addresses in the same bank require multiple service rounds. This differs\n  from global-memory coalescing.\n\nMemory Wall     CPU Memory    GPU Memory    SOTA Memory     Advanced AI Computing and Systems        51",
     "preview_url": "/api/documents/ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402/pages/51/preview",
